#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087578 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/id #uuid "eb0b899e-af3a-587c-96ae-f1da9e0929dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5847" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{4x^3-x}{3x^3+24}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^3.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}-x}{3{x}^{3}+24} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ and $\\dfrac{24}{x^3}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4-0}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = \\dfrac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087579 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/id #uuid "0d53819b-ca24-549d-98e2-a9b78b49c080" :question/math-academy-id "e-5849" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{3x^2+ 2x}{6x^4-1}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^4.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x}{6{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms  $\\dfrac{3}{x^2}, \\dfrac{2}{x^3},$ and $\\dfrac{1}{x^4}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087580 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/id #uuid "ea2a97a7-fce8-5b4a-a269-57e0ae7984d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5851" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x^3}{x^2-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^2.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y = f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f3bea2c-306c-5e48-88e0-f498569d09ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378424} {:answer/id #uuid "10399da4-e690-5c4d-b2a4-9bff2c1171d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378425} {:answer/id #uuid "a1078051-d4e1-5037-957d-9cb4bee113ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378426} {:answer/id #uuid "0308c1f2-6766-5c2d-93c2-954e116c5e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378427} {:answer/id #uuid "c831d6cb-31f4-5404-8eed-6b17d2e24673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10399da4-e690-5c4d-b2a4-9bff2c1171d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378425} :answer-field/id #uuid "219e25d5-1a5d-54bd-84e6-7016995fdf31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4507ae6a-461d-5502-b3e2-7bf57bad8732" :question/math-academy-id "q-113180" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3x+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\frac{2}{x}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ec2ab89-ebdf-50c8-bf29-dc40d7200760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1,\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423002} {:answer/id #uuid "bfdb7b5d-ee60-569b-b40a-75a261258200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423012} {:answer/id #uuid "c06ff70c-7a27-5b2e-868a-5637f1729306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423013} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb0d71f-1a47-548f-bfbd-33f93735b69d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423016} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b97868-31e1-5731-98b7-826c964228e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c06ff70c-7a27-5b2e-868a-5637f1729306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423013} :answer-field/id #uuid "12081ca9-74ed-57c2-90fb-17d28aa1c69d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1258d96-ffcb-5d05-bab8-0035cb3a121b" :question/math-academy-id "q-4002" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+5}{{x}^{2}-x-2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+5}{{x}^{2}-x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{x},\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{1-0-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"}]}
